  "meta": {
    "title": "Molt Rush: Copepod Exoskeleton Fall on the Gulf of California Shelf",
    "date": "2026-11-23",
    "lesson": "Every night, vertically migrating copepods and krill molt their chitinous exoskeletons as they grow, and these molts rain down through the water column as a steady, predictable form of marine snow. Unlike a single dramatic carcass fall, this is a chronic, diffuse nutrient pulse that chitinolytic bacteria exploit by sensing ammonium released as the chitin breaks down (a byproduct of chitin's nitrogen content) and swimming up-gradient toward molts before competitors arrive. Because exoskeletons are tough and proteinaceous as well as chitinous, successful colonizers need both chitinase and protease, not just one enzyme. Twitching motility lets cells grip a sinking molt and ride it down rather than drifting away, but pili are also the receptor phages use, so attached grazers face more risk of infection — a real tradeoff between feeding opportunity and viral vulnerability. The result is a thermocline-bounded community: surface molts decay fast in warm, lit water with heavy grazing, while deeper molts sink into cold, dark, nutrient-rich water where competition is slower but colonization is harder to initiate.",
    "citation": "Based on copepod/krill molt flux studies, Gulf of California, and chitin-particle colonization ecology.",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Diel vertical migration molt flux and chitinolytic Vibrio/Pseudoalteromonas succession, Gulf of California shelf/mesopelagic transition."
  },
